 1 big thing: SpaceX aims for historic rocket catch 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 SpaceX's mega rocket Starship ahead of today's test flight from Starbase, Texas. Photo: Eric Gay/AP SpaceX is about to try to catch both its next-gen Starship rocket and its Super Heavy booster back at their launch pad for the first time, Alex Fitzpatrick reports. 

 A successful attempt would make rocketry history — and mark a huge step toward realizing SpaceX's vision of Starship as a fully reusable, 171-foot space launch behemoth.

 That reusability could dramatically cut launch costs, potentially ushering in a new era of commercial spaceflight. ⏱️ Starship is scheduled to launch from South Texas as early as 5:45 p.m. ET this afternoon, barring weather or mechanical troubles.

 The Super Heavy booster will separate and return for a catch attempt a few minutes after launch. (SpaceX has caught Super Heavy before, but never Starship.) 🛰️ Starship will then orbit Earth about 14 times over 22 hours, while deploying new satellites for SpaceX's Starlink internet service.

 It will then head Earthward, flip itself upright and attempt to land in the huge mechanical "chopstick" arms of its launch tower. ✅ Reality check: A test flight this novel leaves lots of room for things to go wrong.

